        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Magnesia</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">Either December 190 or January 189 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">A plain north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a>, between the rivers Phrygios and Hermos, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> (modern Manisa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> under the consul Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a>, about 30,000 men according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and Appian, perhaps 50,000 by some modern historians; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, about 72,000 according to Livy and Appian, perhaps 50,000 by some modern historians</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">A Roman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> victory. The Seleucid army broke and fled, cities surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> ended Seleucid domination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</span></div>
