    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and Appian agree on about 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and 72,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, but some modern historians think each army may have had about 50,000 men.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes</a> broke the Seleucid left with missile troops and a cavalry charge, while the Seleucid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> fell apart when its elephants panicked under a hail of projectiles.</li><li>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> gave up his claims west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> west of the Taurus went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> gives 53,000 Seleucid dead against 349 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, while modern estimates give 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and 5,000 Romans killed.</p></aside>
    </section>

