    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">196 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> meets Roman envoys at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a>, who demand that he leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and restore the autonomy of the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">192 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> at the urging of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> declares war in November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">191 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> at Thermopylae and, in September, his fleet at Corycus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">190 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A combined Roman and Rhodian fleet defeats the Seleucid fleet at Myonessus, opening the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 190 or January 189 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Scipio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes</a> defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 189 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A truce is signed at Sardes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">189 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> begin the Galatian War, and peace talks are held with the Senate</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">188 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> is signed</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-magnesia" data-v="74cd5273d4" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies line up</b> The two armies lined up facing each other without fighting. The Romans stood with their left towards the rivers, and Antiochus took the Roman challenge on the third day after the last Roman advance.</li><li><b>Eumenes drives off the chariots</b> Eumenes sent forward archers, slingers and spearmen against the scythed chariots. The chariots suffered heavy casualties and fled in panic, throwing the camel archers and cataphracts behind them into confusion.</li><li><b>The Seleucid left is broken</b> Eumenes charged with his cavalry before the cataphracts could reorganise. The Roman and Pergamene cavalry broke through the Seleucid left flank, and the cataphracts fled to the Seleucid camp.</li><li><b>The infantry beside the phalanx give way</b> The Galatians, Cappadocians and mercenary infantry were attacked at once by the Roman centre and right. They retreated, which left the left flank of the phalanx exposed.</li><li><b>Antiochus attacks on the right</b> Antiochus led his cataphracts and agema cavalry against the Latin infantry while the argyraspides engaged the legionaries. The Roman infantry broke ranks and retreated to their camp, where the Thracians and Macedonians reinforced them.</li><li><b>The phalanx breaks</b> The phalanx held against the Roman infantry but could not drive off the archers and slingers who shot at it. It began a slow retreat, the elephants between its units panicked, and the phalangites threw down their weapons and left the field.</li><li><b>Antiochus withdraws</b> Antiochus's cavalry came back to reinforce the centre after his army had already dispersed. He gathered the surviving troops and retreated to Sardes.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip V of Macedon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Philip_V_of_Macedon_in_Palazzo_Massimo_(Rome).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Livioandronico2013, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-syrian-war">Fifth Syrian War</a> in 198 BCE, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>. He then turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. In 196 BCE he used the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> to attack cities held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalid dynasty</a>, and his troops crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> to rebuild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a>. Smyrna and Lampsacus, fearing that he would take all of Asia Minor, appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for protection.</p><p>At Lysimachia in October 196 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> demanded that Antiochus withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and restore the autonomy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> of Asia Minor. He answered that he was rebuilding the empire of his ancestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-ii-theos">Antiochus II Theos</a> and criticised Roman meddling in states whose rights <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> had traditionally defended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> fled to Antiochus' court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in the winter of 196 and 195 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> promised Antiochus its support in 193 BCE and then seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a>, and in September 192 BCE the Aetolian general Thoantas persuaded him to oppose the Romans openly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>The war went against Antiochus. He was defeated at Thermopylae in April 191 BCE, his fleet lost at Corycus and Myonessus, and he lost control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. He offered to pay half the Roman war expenses and to accept the demands made at Lysimachia, but the Romans were by then determined to crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> once and for all.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and Appian both give the Roman army as about 30,000 men and the Seleucid army as approximately 72,000. Modern historians disagree: some accept the figures of the primary sources, while others hold that each army may have numbered some 50,000. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had 16 African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, considered inferior to the Asian elephants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, who had 54. The Romans were led by the consul Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus and were joined by the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a>' left wing was commanded by his son Seleucus and his nephew Antipater, and he led the right himself. The centre was a Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> of 16,000 under Philip, the master of the elephants, drawn up in ten taxeis, each 50 men wide and 32 deep, with elephants in pairs in the gaps. Ahead of the left flank stood scythed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-borne Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, with 6,000 light infantry beside them. Seven thousand of the least combat-ready troops guarded the camp.</p><p>On the Roman side the legate Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus held the left with 10,800 heavy infantry, and Scipio the centre with another 10,800 in three lines, in a more open and flexible formation than their adversaries. Eumenes held the right with 2,800 to 3,000 cavalry, mostly Roman, with an 800-man <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> force. Light infantry and archers stood ahead of the main force, and 2,000 Thracian and Macedonian volunteers with the elephants formed the rearguard.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Magnesia&#8221; cites where it covers Armies:</p><ul><li>Michael Taylor, Antiochus The Great (Pen and Sword Military, 2013)</li><li>John Grainger, The Roman War of Antiochus the Great (Brill, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> towards Thyatira, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> camped 15 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a>, and the Romans camped 6.5 kilometres from him, north of the Hermos. They refused to be lured out by a detachment of 1,000 Galatian and Dahae cavalry, then moved to a horseshoe-shaped plain about 4 kilometres from the Seleucid camp, enclosed on three sides by the Phrygios and the Hermos. For five days the armies lined up without fighting. Scipio could not storm a fortified camp and wished to win before a new consul replaced him. The Romans advanced to where the Phrygios turned north, leaving their right flank unprotected, and Antiochus accepted battle at dawn on the third day after.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, slingers and spearmen against the scythed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, which fled in panic and threw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> archers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a> behind them into confusion. He then charged with his cavalry before the cataphracts could reorganise, broke through the Seleucid left and drove them to the camp.</p><p>On the other wing Antiochus attacked with his cataphracts and agema cavalry and the argyraspides, and the Roman infantry broke and fell back to their camp, where they were reinforced and rallied by the tribune Marcus Aemilius Lepidus. Antiochus' cavalry became bogged down at the camp. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> held but could not dislodge the archers and slingers bombarding it. As it began a slow, organised retreat its elephants panicked and broke its formation. The phalangites discarded their weapons and left the field, and Antiochus' army had dispersed before his cavalry returned.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Magnesia&#8221; cites where it covers Battle:</p><ul><li>Bezalel Bar-Kochva, The Seleucid Army: Organization and Tactics in the Great Campaigns (Cambridge University Press, 1976)</li><li>John Grainger, The Roman War of Antiochus the Great (Brill, 2002)</li><li>Michael Taylor, Antiochus The Great (Pen and Sword Military, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, 53,000 Seleucid soldiers died, 1,400 were captured and 15 elephants were taken, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> lost 349 men with many more wounded. Modern estimates give 10,000 dead for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> and 5,000 killed for the Romans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> gathered the survivors and retreated to Sardes while the Romans were busy looting his camp. There he learnt that his son Seleucus had survived, and he went to Apamea to meet him.</p><p>The defeat and the withdrawal of the Seleucid fleet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patara">Patara</a> led the garrisons of Sardes, Ephesus, Thyatira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a> itself to surrender to the Romans. Antiochus sent Zeuxis and Antipater to seek a truce, which was signed at Sardes in January 189 BCE. He agreed to abandon his claims to all lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, pay a heavy indemnity and hand over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and other notable enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> among his allies.</p><p>The Romans began the Galatian War to subjugate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and punish Antiochus' allies, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> they suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athamanians">Athamanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>, who had broken the terms of an earlier truce. In the summer of 189 BCE envoys of the Seleucids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and other states held peace talks with the Senate. Lycia and Caria were given to Rhodes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and most of Asia Minor west of the Taurus. Antiochus also had to hand over Hannibal, Thoantas and twenty notables as hostages, keep only ten ships, and give Rome 40,500 modiuses of grain a year. The terms took effect with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in the summer of 188 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The two main historical accounts of the battle are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>'s Ab Urbe Condita Libri and Appian's Syriaca. They agree on the sizes of the armies, about 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and some 72,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, but modern historians dispute those figures, and some think each army may have numbered about 50,000. The same gap separates the casualty figures: Livy has 53,000 Seleucid dead and 349 Roman, against modern estimates of 10,000 and 5,000.</p><p>The date is also uncertain, since the battle took place either in December 190 or January 189 BCE. The story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> whether his army would be enough for the Romans, and Hannibal answered that it was quite enough for the Romans, however greedy they were, is called a popular anecdote.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was a decisive Roman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> victory, and it led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a>, which ended Seleucid domination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> gave up his claims west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> were the main gainers. Lycia and Caria went to Rhodes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and most of Asia Minor west of the Taurus to the Attalids, while the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> that had sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> before the battle was guaranteed.</p><p>Magnesia also marked the end of the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>'s dominance on the battlefields of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. The Seleucid phalanx held its ground against the Roman infantry, but it was not mobile enough to answer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and slingers, and its own elephants broke it. The battlefield itself had been used before, for the Seleucid victory at Corupedium in 281 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a>, in December 190 or January 189 BCE, Lucius Cornelius Scipio Asiaticus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>'s army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> gives 53,000 Seleucid dead, modern estimates 10,000. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> ended Seleucid domination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</em></p>

